So he said, you know, let me teach you a little bit about the subscription business that you're building.

22
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:20,640
He went to the whiteboard and he basically explained to me this idea that as subscription businesses get older, as their cohorts age, the churn just kind of naturally comes down over time.

23
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:26,240
And it was a very simple chart accompanied with like thirty or forty five seconds of voice over.

24
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:35,040
And it actually just became something of a life of its own where, you know, I took that idea and whenever I would be closing a candidate, I would walk them through that idea.

25
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:38,160
Whenever I was pitching investors, I would walk them through that idea.

26
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:42,240
And it was like, it was one chart on the whiteboard and then a little bit of a voiceover.

27
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:46,560
And it really struck me, you know, many years later, that every business has something like this.

28
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:52,880
You know, every business has like, you know, to really understand the nuance, there are like two or three things that you just need to understand.

29
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:55,440
And it's one chart and a little bit of a voiceover.

30
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:58,560
And so that was actually the inspiration for the first screenshot essay.

31
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:14,560
Could I destill this idea that I've been telling people so many times over and over down to like one chart and thirty or forty five seconds of voiceoverice over and that concurrently dovetailed with kind of this feeling that I had was that business writing writ large was just kind of trying to boil the ocean.

32
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:17,760
So could I distill something down to like a minute or less?

33
