66
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:10,160
And I kind of copy an old slide, make a new slide and just start thinking through the chart.

67
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:18,240
And then I found that once I get the chart down, everything flows from there whenever I have a writer's block, it's not writer's block, it's chart creation block or something.

68
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,720
And then once I can break through that, I start to write around it.

69
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:38,320
But, you know, all these things that I've written about really are, um, things that I find myself saying to other people, whether they're businesses that I've invested in, um, you know, businesses that we've bought through Universe Software, a business that I've built, this kind of core essence of what drives the business.

70
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:44,160
And then when I find myself explaining it to someone like three or four years ago, I've talked to someone like three or four times, I'm like, okay, I should probably write an essay about this.

71
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:50,960
I mean, it's a skill you have to hone as an investor sounding smart in like a 30-minute meeting, right?

72
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:52,480
Exactly, exactly.

73
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:53,600
We don't Yeah, exactly.

74
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:55,760
We talk for like 10% of the time with the entrepreneur.

75
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:59,040
So when we talk, we, we got to get right to the point and say something impressive.

76
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:01,760
It's always, it's always harder than it seems.

77
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:10,480
I think applicable to our audience, and then part of the reason I wanted to talk about this is that, and I feel like we need to do this for revenue, Cat.

78
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:11,680
Like we need to do a better job.

79
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:14,320
A new product marketing manager started today.

80
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:19,280
The marketing team, we've talked a ton about different kinds of positioning things.

81
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:36,080
And like, I think for most apps out there, for almost anyone listening to this podcast who is running a business, like, try and make a screenshot essay of like what's meaningful or what's, you know, what distribution advantage you have, what, what's unique about your business?

82
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:43,600
And boiling it down is, if you guys want me to look, if the marketing team wants me to do more content, just tell me if it's stuck to a size ofze of a screen.

83
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:44,960
You're welcome any time, Jacob.

84
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:48,720
You're welcome any time on screenchat essays dot com dot You're welcome.

85
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:48,880
Okay.

86
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:52,640
I think you hit the three ideas that I have, I think you already wrote about.

87
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:53,520
So, like, I don't know.

88
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:54,320
I'll get perfect.

89
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:56,160
Be creative, but yeah.

90
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:57,680
I mean, it's true though.

91
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:04,080
It's, I mean, what's there, like, overused Mark Twain quote, like, I would have written you a shorter letter, but I didn't have enough time.

92
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:08,320
It's really, it's actually really challenging to write.

93
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:12,480
And you have to, you have to be really comfortable not saying things, right?

94
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:18,000
I think it's really, it's a really., you have to have a lot of confidence to be like, okay, I'm going to leave that out.

95
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:18,960
I'm going to leave this out.

96
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,120
I'm going to kind of assume that the reader is going to follow this.

97
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:24,640
And they may or they may not, right?

98
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:32,720
It's a fine, yeah, it's a tough, it's a tough game to balance leaving out the context but like still maintaining the overall point of the message.

99
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:34,000
100%.

100
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:44,400
And just to bring it back to, you know, developers and those building companies, like whenever I write these essays, there are always points that I'm like, oh man, like, I didn't want to cut that, right?

101
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:44,960
But you do.

102
00:07:45,280 --> 00:07:47,440
And I think the same is true in building any company, right?

103
00:07:47,680 --> 00:08:00,880
Whenever you're pitching the company again to investors, to the press, to people you're going to hire, there's like ten talking points that you talk through, that you talk through, and you see someone's eyes light up and you hate to lose eight or nine of those, but often that's what you need to do, right?

104
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:04,320
To really land the punch on the one thing, the one point you're trying to make.

105
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:06,400
And with my essay, I find that all the time.

106
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:08,960
It's just, okay, what am I actually trying to say here?

107
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:10,240
What's the one takeaway?

108
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:14,640
Because in, you know, in one screenshot, you don't have much time to jump all over the place.

109
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:18,480
Well, that's a great lesson for product development too.

110
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:20,880
I mean, you know, that's the constant battle.

111
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:26,320
It's like there's ten features you always want to build and all of them are great, but what's that?

112
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,400
Those one or two you need to focus on right now.

113
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:35,600
Yeah, only two or three will matter and most people can only really understand two or three, right, about your product, at least in that initial pitch, right?

114
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:37,840
When you're building a platform or something, maybe it changes.

115
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:46,240
And it's so painful to an entrepreneur because you're like, I spent so much time on these other ten things and like maybe these other ten things were so much harder to build than the two things that you actually want to hear about.

116
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:46,560
Yeah.

117
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:48,560
But I have to put that aside, right?

118
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:51,360
Well, let's dive into the meat of this then.

119
00:08:52,320 --> 00:09:09,520
I think this is one of the first screenshot essays I saw of yours, or at least one early one that lit up my mind, being so focused on consumer subscriptions myself, was the one where you talked about Duolingo, Bumble, and the rise of consumer SaaS and how it parallels B2B SaaS.

120
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:18,320
So I'd love if you gave us just kind of a, I mean, you could almost read the whole screenshot essay and it'd be as not.

121
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:19,440
Come on, grotesque.

122
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:20,240
Exactly.

123
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:26,560
So give us a little summary of your ideas there, and then I'd love to ask some questions about your thinking.

124
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:28,000
Yeah, for sure.

125
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:30,080
And I'll put some context around it.

126
00:09:30,960 --> 00:09:44,640
It really was written as a rallying cry for those building in consumer SaaS to say, hey, you know, I know people might be overlooking your business or underestimating it, but these businesses can grow much larger than anyone thinks.

127
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:46,640
And you should have confidence in that.

128
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:53,920
And so what gave me the confidence to write that is that that I started my career in 2010 working actually at a consumer internet company.

129
00:09:54,560 --> 00:10:00,480
But at the time, I vividly remember that all the oxygen in the room, all anyone was talking about were consumer companies.

130
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:01,440
It was Google.

131
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:02,400
It was Amazon.

132
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:04,560
It was Facebook, which was on the rise.

133
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:12,320
And in fact, if you as an entrepreneur said, I'm building a SaaS business, people would look at you like a step up from building a lemonade stand, right?

134
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:14,720
It was like, wow, you must not be ambitious.

135
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:15,840
You're building a SaaS company.

136
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:16,400
I remember that.

137
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:17,520
And I had an SDK.

138
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:21,360
I had an SDK company doing something not that dissimilar from RevenueCat.

139
00:10:21,440 --> 00:10:23,760
And like all the advice we got was like just grow your uses.

140
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:31,200
Like everyone was pitching us like a if we had just like a SAS fee on it, whatever made it a SAS company, oh, we'd be in a different ever.

141
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:32,160
But yeah, totally.

142
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:32,320
Yeah.

143
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:34,000
It's what and it made sense.

144
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:35,520
Like the data supported it.

145
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:35,680
Right.

146
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:46,560
So like Google in 2010 was a 300 billion dollar company and then Salesforce, which was like the best SAS business, you know, after, you know, ten years of building, it's a 10 billion dollar company.

147
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:46,880
Right.

148
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:50,720
And so and then there weren't many even billion dollar SAS businesses.

149
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:54,720
And so like the data supported kind of the narrative that everybody was saying.

150
00:10:55,040 --> 00:10:58,560
And obviously, if you fell into that narrative, you just got it completely wrong, right?

151
00:10:58,800 --> 00:11:04,320
So fast forward to today, in many ways, the most interesting businesses of this generation are all SaaS companies, right?

152
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:06,640
There's Shopify, Zoom, Square, et cetera.

153
00:11:07,280 --> 00:11:14,000
And so what people got wrong was they were comparing two different types of businesses that were actually on different evolutionary timelines.

154
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:25,680
And so the kind of Internet giants of Web 1.0 came up a little earlier than a lot of these SaaS companies and the SaaS companiesies came up later with kind of later built out infrastructure.

155
00:11:25,920 --> 00:11:29,280
So different evolutionary timeline playing out on different time scales.

156
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:44,160
And so I made the comparison to consumer SaaS today, where, you know, SaaS has had the benefit of twenty, twenty five years of this infrastructure being built out of companies building companies or people building companies and people learning how to build those companies.

157
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:47,280
And consumer SaaS by comparison is just way newer.

158
00:11:47,520 --> 00:11:51,600
And so, you know, Apple only launched an app subscription in 2011.

159
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:56,480
Like, I know you all know that, but like, a lot of people don't focus on it.

160
00:11:57,280 --> 00:11:58,400
And then I'm very limited audience.

161
00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:02,000
And then Stripe, same thing, around the same time, and Google, the same time.

162
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:07,600
So, like, it's new in this, in the spectrum of internet evolution, like, it's very, very new.

163
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:14,080
So the point I was making that essay is, um, consumer SaaS is on a different, later evolutionary timeline than SaaS.

164
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:18,720
It should not be compared to it in terms of the overall market caps or sizes of these businesses.

165
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:22,160
It should not be dismissed out of hand as just inherently smaller.

166
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:23,840
We need to give it some time to play out.

167
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:31,520
Let's check the receipts in ten years when some of these businesses like Discord, like Duolingo, have another ten years of growth behind them.

168
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:49,600
So I love that the goal behind this was to be a rallying cry, but I, but I have to ask, you know, because these are the things I think about is, you know, B2B SaaS now, you know, multiple hundred billion dollar companies and and lots of Decacorns and like hundreds of unicorns it seemed.

169
00:12:49,920 --> 00:13:03,120
But part of the reason for that is that you were creating a ton of value to businesses who are not shy about paying up, you know, to to save employees time to increase productivity to grow the business.

170
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:16,000
And so in some ways it makes sense, you know, how profitable those SaaS businesses are, but I still wonder like in consumer SaaS, is it really that big of an opportunity?

171
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:17,760
Like are consumers willing to pay?

172
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:22,080
You know, can we make it up on volume in consumer SaaS?

173
00:13:22,160 --> 00:13:27,680
Like what are your thoughts about like how consumer SaaS is gets to be that big.

174
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:28,480
Yeah.

175
00:13:28,880 --> 00:13:31,040
So I'll come to this from a few different directions.

176
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:37,600
Number one is just the starting point, which is that consumer spending in the United States is like two thirds of spend.

177
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:40,160
So it's like greater than business spend, right?

178
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:41,120
So you start there.

179
00:13:41,440 --> 00:13:45,920
Now, of course, there's a lot of things people are spending on that aren't ultimately going to be captured by apps.

180
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:53,760
But just as a starting point, there's no inherent reason why consumer SaaS or consumer spend should be dramatically less than B2B spending.

181
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:55,520
So that's kind of my starting point.

182
00:13:55,680 --> 00:13:56,640
I think the second point is.

183
00:13:57,280 --> 00:14:05,520
the way these things play out over time of how businesses can find a wedge and then expand into adjacent areas is just dramatically underestimated.

184
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:15,840
And so if you look at something like Shopify, which today is kind of the hallmark example of a phenomenal SaaS business that has expanded for the first ten years of Shopify's life, it was SaaS only.

185
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:18,960
It wasn't until 2013 that they launched payments.

186
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:20,880
And then a few years later, they launched Capital.

187
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:23,920
And more recently, they launched banking and cards.

188
00:14:24,480 --> 00:14:29,520
And today, fintech or merchant services is roughly seventy percent of its revenue.

189
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:29,920
Right.

190
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:34,240
And so if you had said in 2011, like, what shop if I'm going to become, how big can it be?

191
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:43,200
You would really only be thinking about the SaaS opportunity and you'd be actually missing the vast majority of the opportunity that they would pursue over the next ten years.

192
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:49,600
And so part of this is a part of it is, well, consumer spending is the majority of spend in the United States.

193
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:57,520
The other one is just the belief that I have in these kind of innovation cycles that I've seen play out many times over, which are entrepreneurs find a wedge.

194
00:14:57,680 --> 00:15:04,880
They then find creativative and interesting ways to expand on that wedge through new infrastructure that pops up to enable them.

195
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:13,440
And then there's this beautiful cycle between more applications being built, more products being built, more infrastructure being built to serve those use cases and on and on and on.

196
00:15:13,680 --> 00:15:20,000
And so part of it, again, is just this belief that that innovation cycle is still in flight within consumer subscription.

197
00:15:20,640 --> 00:15:20,720
Yeah.

